 ### [ENeRF with Guided Sampling Training](7580-enerf-with-guided-sampling-training.md)
-Integrates pixel-region and depth-boundary-guided ray sampling into the Efficient NeRF framework to reduce GPU memory consumption and improve sampling efficiency compared to uniform strategies.
+Integrates pixel-region and depth-boundary-guided ray sampling into ENeRF to reduce GPU memory and improve sampling efficiency using monocular depth priors.
+
+### [Grid-Guided NeRF Branch Training](17510-grid-guided-nerf-branch-training.md)
+Uses a discrete feature grid to guide NeRF sampling toward the scene surface, resolving shape-radiance ambiguity in large-scale urban scenes.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:** The method adds overhead for computing multi-view consistency error maps (projection and interpolation) and requires storing adapted depth priors. The sampling step is slightly more complex than uniform sampling due to adaptive bounds, but the number of samples $M$ remains the same. The core volume rendering complexity is unchanged [S1].
-**Measured Performance:** On the ScanNet dataset, the method outperforms state-of-the-art depth estimation methods in all metrics, including those trained with ground truth depth supervision (e.g., DeepV2D, DELTAS, Atlas) [S1]. It significantly improves depth estimation quality over original NeRF, particularly in indoor scenes [S1].
-**Strengths:** Effectively mitigates shape-radiance ambiguity in poorly textured indoor areas (e.g., walls) where standard NeRF fails [S1]. It allows accurate depth estimation by directly optimizing over RGB images without requiring explicit geometric loss terms during training [S1].
-**Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the adapted monocular depth priors. Applying existing correspondence-based neural optimization (like CVD) to these priors can degrade quality due to unreliable flow correspondences in textureless regions [S1].
-**When to use:** Indoor multi-view stereo and depth estimation where texture is sparse and shape-radiance ambiguity is high [S1].
-**When not to use:** Scenes with rich texture where standard NeRF sampling is sufficient, or when high-quality monocular depth priors are not available [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The method adds overhead to the standard NeRF training loop due to the initial multi-view consistency check (projection of depth maps) and the final bilateral filtering. The core training loop complexity remains similar to NeRF, $O(N_{rays} \cdot M)$, but the adaptive sampling may reduce the effective search space, potentially accelerating convergence in ambiguous regions [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing $N$ depth maps and $N$ error maps. The projection step requires temporary storage for projected coordinates and depths. No significant additional memory is required for the NeRF model itself compared to standard NeRF [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Outperforms original NeRF and other state-of-the-art methods on indoor datasets like ScanNet in terms of depth estimation accuracy (e.g., AbsRel, SqRel) and view synthesis quality (PSNR) [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Effectively mitigates shape-radiance ambiguity in poorly textured indoor areas (e.g., walls) where standard NeRF fails. Does not require ground-truth depth supervision during training [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the initial monocular depth priors. Performance degrades if the priors are inaccurate. The method assumes sufficient multi-view overlap for the consistency check [S1].
+*   **When to use:** Indoor multi-view stereo and depth estimation where texture is sparse and ground-truth depth is unavailable.
+*   **When not to use:** Outdoor scenes with rich textures where standard NeRF sampling is sufficient, or scenes with significant motion blur where monocular priors are unreliable [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method was proposed in "Depth-Guided Optimization of Neural Radiance Fields for Indoor Multi-View Stereo" [S1]. It addresses the limitation of original NeRF [33], which assumes that correct geometry is favored due to smoother surface light fields, an assumption that fails in poorly textured indoor environments [S1]. The authors observed that NeRF can synthesize high-quality RGB images with incorrect geometry in such areas, leading to depth deviation from ground truth [S1]. The method builds on adapted monocular depth priors (finetuned on the target scene) and guides the NeRF sampling process to avoid degenerate cases [S1].
+The method was introduced in "Depth-Guided Optimization of Neural Radiance Fields for Indoor Multi-View Stereo" [S1]. It addresses the limitation of standard NeRF, which suffers from shape-radiance ambiguity in indoor environments, particularly in texture-less regions like walls [S1]. While NeRF can synthesize high-quality RGB images, the underlying geometry (depth) often deviates significantly from the ground truth [S1]. The authors observed that applying correspondence-based optimization directly to monocular depth priors degrades quality, motivating the guided optimization approach that uses the priors to constrain sampling rather than as direct supervision [S1].
 
